# AGENTS.md — <your name / project> global context

<!--
This is the top-of-stack context file. It loads every session, before you type
anything, so it costs attention on every turn. Keep it SHORT: a contract and an
index, not a knowledge base. Topics longer than ~2 lines move down into their
own rules/ file and get a one-line pointer here. See docs/01-rules-and-context.

Canonical filename is AGENTS.md at every layer (user-global, machine, project),
with CLAUDE.md as a symlink to it for tools that still look for the older name.
-->

## Identity

- **Name:** <your name>
- **Role:** <what you do, one line>
- **How I work:** <one or two lines — e.g. "ship and iterate, direct comms, technical depth matters">

## Hard defaults

<!-- The handful of rules true everywhere, every session, that are short enough to live here. -->

- <language/tooling default — e.g. "Python: always `uv`, never global pip">
- <a destructive-op guardrail — e.g. "never force push; use `trash`, not `rm -rf`">
- <an asking-vs-acting default — e.g. "ask before anything public-facing or irreversible">

## Rules index

<!-- Each topic with real depth gets its own rules/<topic>.md that auto-loads.
     This section is just the table of contents. Update it when a rule file changes. -->

- `rules/voice.md` — how I write (the canonical style guide for all comms)
- `rules/security.md` — secrets, git, destructive ops
- `rules/model-selection.md` — which model/agent for which task
- `rules/<topic>.md` — <one-line description>

## Hierarchy

1. **User-global** (this file + `rules/*.md`) — applies everywhere
2. **Machine-local** (`~/AGENTS.md` per host) — per-host facts
3. **Project** (`<project>/AGENTS.md`) — per-repo conventions

Each layer inherits from the one above; the more specific layer wins on conflict.

## Why this file stays short

Every standing instruction competes with the actual task for the model's focus.
A rule that helps one task in fifty is a tax on the other forty-nine. Prune by
relevance-per-turn and by conflict, not toward a line count. When a rule stops
earning its turn-by-turn cost, move it down a layer or cut it. A specific fact
you learned once is a memory (see the memory store), not a rule.
